# HANDOFF — David's Website

**Last updated:** 2026-09-18  
**Target domain:** `tilleyresearchgroup.com`

## 1. Project objective

Replace the current Tilley Research Group website with a static website that is easy to maintain and eventually host on GitHub Pages while retaining the existing custom domain.

The most important requirement is long-term maintainability: future group members should update people, alumni, research, news, publications, positions, etc. by editing simple Markdown files, without editing HTML/CSS or understanding the site implementation.

**First instruction for the next Codex agent:** inspect the actual project/repository before changing anything. Do not assume previously proposed paths were actually implemented.

## 2. Design philosophy

The intended model is:

- one person = one Markdown file;
- one news item = one Markdown file;
- one research topic = one Markdown file;
- one position = one Markdown file;
- metadata/frontmatter controls where an item appears;
- templates generate the actual pages/cards.

For example:

```md
---
name: Jane Doe
position: postdoc
photo: /images/people/jane-doe.jpg
email: jane.doe@example.com
order: 20
---

Jane works on ...
```

Changing:

```yaml
position: postdoc
```

to:

```yaml
position: alumni
```

should automatically move that person from the current-members section to Alumni after the site rebuilds. No HTML list should need manual editing.

## 3. Why a static-site generator was discussed

Plain HTML was considered, but manually maintaining separate HTML pages/cards would undermine the desired Markdown-driven handover.

Astro and Jekyll were therefore discussed.

A generator is a **build tool**, not a separate public hosting service:

```text
Markdown + templates + CSS + images
                |
                v
          Astro/Jekyll
                |
                v
        HTML + CSS + JS
                |
                v
          GitHub Pages
                |
                v
        visitor's browser
```

Visitors receive generated static files. Astro/Jekyll do not need to run in the visitor's browser.

## 4. Astro discussion

Astro was considered because it offers clean content organization, Markdown/content collections, reusable components, and static output.

A conceptual structure previously proposed was:

```text
content/
  people/
  research/
  news/
  publications/
  positions/

public/
  images/

src/
  ...
```

This was a proposal, **not a verified description of the current project**.

## 5. Jekyll discussion

Jekyll was also considered because it is Markdown-oriented and historically integrates simply with GitHub Pages.

A conceptual prototype structure proposed earlier was:

```text
_people/
  person-1.md
  person-2.md

_layouts/
  ...

assets/
  ...

_config.yml
index.html
people.html
```

Jekyll would generate `_site/`.

Again, this was a proposed architecture, not confirmation that these files currently exist.

## 6. Framework decision

Do not migrate between Astro/Jekyll merely for architectural purity.

The success criterion is:

> Can a future group member safely maintain the website by editing obvious Markdown files?

If the current offline implementation already uses a workable architecture, continue with it unless there is a concrete reason to change it.

## 7. Hosting and custom domain

The intended final architecture is:

```text
Git repository
      |
      v
static-site build
      |
      v
GitHub Pages
      |
      v
tilleyresearchgroup.com
```

The current custom domain should be retained.

Before migration, identify:

1. domain registrar;
2. DNS provider;
3. current DNS records;
4. current hosting destination;
5. who has credentials/authority to modify DNS.

Do not change DNS during development.

Safe migration sequence:

```text
build new site
      ↓
test offline
      ↓
test on temporary GitHub Pages URL
      ↓
compare against current site
      ↓
verify links/assets/mobile layout
      ↓
configure custom domain
      ↓
change DNS
      ↓
verify HTTPS/domain
      ↓
only then retire old hosting
```

The existing public site should remain online until the replacement is validated.

