## 11. Best diagnostic procedure

After listing the repository tree, choose a distinctive piece of visible content from the offline website, such as a person's name or a paragraph, and search the whole project for that exact string.

This quickly reveals whether the content originates in:

- Markdown;
- HTML;
- JSON/data;
- JavaScript;
- a template;
- generated output only.

Then trace the path from source content to generated HTML.

## 12. Source files vs generated files

The repository must make this distinction obvious.

An Astro-style example:

```text
src/content/people/alice.md       <- EDIT THIS
src/pages/...                     <- template/code

dist/people/index.html            <- GENERATED; DO NOT EDIT
```

A Jekyll-style example:

```text
_people/alice.md                  <- EDIT THIS
people.html                       <- template

_site/people/index.html           <- GENERATED; DO NOT EDIT
```

These are examples only. Use the actual paths discovered in the repository.

Generated HTML may be inspected for debugging, but should not become the normal content-editing interface.

## 13. Recommended content/presentation separation

Frequently edited content:

```text
people
research
news
publications
positions
contact information
images
```

Rarely edited implementation:

```text
layouts
components
CSS
navigation logic
build configuration
deployment configuration
```

The intended maintenance workflow is:

```text
open Markdown file
        ↓
change text/metadata
        ↓
save/commit
        ↓
automatic build
        ↓
website updates
```

## 14. Suggested frontmatter convention

If the existing implementation has no schema, use a consistent one. Example:

```md
---
name: "Jane Doe"
slug: "jane-doe"
position: "postdoc"
title: "Postdoctoral Researcher"
photo: "/images/people/jane-doe.jpg"
email: "jane.doe@example.com"
order: 20
active: true
---

Research description here.
```

Possible normalized role values:

```text
pi
postdoc
phd
master
visitor
staff
alumni
```

Templates should map these values to sections rather than requiring section information to be duplicated in HTML.

## 15. Images

Keep images separate from Markdown and reference them by stable paths.

Conceptually:

```text
public/images/people/
public/images/research/
public/images/news/
```

Use whatever equivalent path the actual framework expects.

Prefer descriptive filenames such as:

```text
jane-doe.jpg
group-photo-2026.jpg
```

## 16. URLs

Preserve important existing public routes where practical, especially routes already indexed or externally linked.

Before launch:

1. inventory important URLs on the current site;
2. compare them with new routes;
3. preserve paths where practical;
4. create redirects where the hosting setup supports them;
5. verify navigation and discoverability.

Do not casually rename public routes immediately before migration.

